1.1.6.m:
When one establishes the fire,
he should offer afterwards an eleven-pot-sherd cake to Indra and Agni,
and a boiled rice mess to Aditi.
Indra and Agni are the unexhausted ones among the gods;
whichever deities are unexhausted,
from both of them he secures sensual power and heroic power for him.
It belongs to Aditi;
Aditi is this [earth];
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth].
This is the semen of the milch cow,

namely the clarified butter;
the rice grains belong to the draft ox;
he secures a pair.
It is in ghee,
so that the sacrifice does not become dry.
Four descendants of ṛṣis
eat it;
he offers [it] in the light of the quarters of space.
These oblations are domestic animals;
this is Rudra,
namely Agni.

just as one would fill three vessels,
such would that be;
he would not leave any room for procreation.
Having offered one,
he should combine the subsequent two;
he leaves a third realm for him for procreation;
he procreates after it with offspring and domestic animals.
Moreover, this is the forward stride of the sacrifice.
He rolls forward a chariot wheel;
by means of the human chariot he mounts down to the divine chariot.

He pleases the seasons, who have Agni as their mouth.
He gives a headrest,
for the obtaining of forms;
a horse to the brahman priest,
he secures sensual power;
a milch cow to the hotṛ,
he secures blessings;
a draft ox to the adhvaryu;
the draft ox is a draft-beast;
the adhvaryu is a draft-beast.

By means of a draft-beast he secures the draft-beast of the sacrifice.
He gives a pair of cattle,
for the obtaining of a pair.
He gives a garment;
the garment belongs to all the gods;
he pleases all the gods.
He gives up to twelve [gifts];
twelve months are the year;
he firmly establishes himself in the year.
At will more may be given,
for the obtaining of the boundless.

1.1.7.m:
'Heat is the head: so this Agni;
may he become well-beloved by the domestic animals;
extend protection to offspring and descendants.
Wind is exhalation: so this Agni;
may he become well-beloved by the domestic animals;
cook seasoned food for offspring and descendants.
Go forth along the eastern direction, knowing,
O Agni, be Agni in front of Agni here;
shining, shine out in all regions;
bestow vigor upon our biped and quadruped.'

1.1.8.m:
These worlds indeed are these fires.
If they were established without distinction,
they would cause suffering to the sacrificer.
With 'Heat is the head,' he establishes the gārhapatya;
with 'Wind is exhalation,' the anvāhāryapacana;
with 'The sun is the eye,' the āhavanīya.
Thereby he distinguishes them;
thus they do not cause suffering to the sacrificer.
He sings the Rathantara over the gārhapatya as it is being established;
this world belongs to the Rathantara.

He establishes it firmly established in this world.
He sings the Vāmadevya as it is being carried out;
the intermediate space is the Vāmadevya;
he establishes it firmly established in the intermediate space.
Moreover, the Vāmadevya is appeasement;
appeased, rich in cattle, he carries it out.
He sings the Bṛhat over the āhavanīya as it is being established;
yonder world belongs to the Bṛhat;
he establishes it firmly established in yonder world.
Prajāpati created Agni.

1.2.1.m:
a leaf fell from the third heaven;
that is this parṇa tree, born indeed from the soma-leaf;
from it I take [a stick] for obtaining the drinking of soma.
When you listened to the gods
speaking the discussion of sacred lore, you are indeed renowned as Suśravas;
from that let spiritual preeminence enter me;
bringing it together, may I obtain that perceptibly.
By which, when Agni was created,
Prajāpati appeased his weapon,
that very [branch of] śamī for non-burning,

1.3.2.m:
He who, knowing thus, sacrifices with the Vājapeya
goes to independent sovereignty;
he attains the foremost place among his equals;
they give way to him for preeminence.
Indeed, this is the sacrifice of both the Brahmin and the Rājanya.
Indeed, they call it the Vājapeya.
Indeed, this is attainable through vigor;
for by this the gods sought to obtain vigor.
The Vājapeya is indeed Soma.
He who knows Soma as the Vājapeya,

having drunk it, he becomes vigorous indeed;
a vigorous one is born to him.
The Vājapeya is indeed food.
He who knows thus
eats food;
an eater of food is born to him.
The Vājapeya is indeed the brahman.
He who knows thus
eats food through the brahman;
a knower of brahman is born to him.

Therefore, one should not accept [gifts] from a singer or an intoxicated person.
If one should accept,
one would accept impurity.
All speeches indeed are obtained for him
who is a sacrificer with the Vājapeya:
that which is on earth, in Agni, in the Rathantara;
that which is in the intermediate space, in Vāyu, in the Vāmadevya;
that which is in heaven, in the Sun, in the Bṛhat;
that which is in the waters, in the plants, in the trees.
Therefore, the sacrificer with the Vājapeya is fit for the priesthood;
for all speeches are obtained for him.

1.3.7.m:
"May I be united with offspring, may offspring be united with me," he says;
this very blessing he seeks.
They strike [him] with small packages of grain;
this [earth] is food;
they enrich him with food.
They strike [him] with salty earth;
for these are visibly food,
namely, the salty earths;
they visibly enrich him with food.
They strike [him] from the front towards the west;

for food is eaten from the front towards the back.
They strike [him] from the head;
for food is eaten from the head.
They strike [him] from the quarters;
from the quarters they secure food for him.
He is liable to perish downwards,
who mounts the sacrificial post.
He steps down onto gold;
gold is immortality;
the heavenly realm is immortality;
he is established in the immortal, heavenly realm.
It weighs a hundred [measures];
man has a hundred-year lifespan, a hundred faculties;
he is established in lifespan, in faculty.
This indeed is the form of nourishment,
namely, a goat;
she gives birth three times a year, beyond other domestic animals.
He steps down onto a he-goat skin;
he is established in nourishment, in procreation.

1.3.8.m:
He offers seven food-oblations;
there are seven foods;
as many foods as there are,
those very ones he secures.
Seven are cultivated plants,
seven are wild;
for the securing of both.
He offers oblation of every food,
for the securing of every food.
If the performer of the Vājapeya should eat of what has not been secured,

he would be deprived of what has been secured.
Having cut off a portion from each, he offers,
for the securing of what has not been secured.
He offers with a ladle of udumbara wood;
the udumbara is vigor, food;
for the securing of vigor, of food.
"At the instigation of divine Savitṛ," he says;
impelled by Savitṛ, he sprinkles him with brahman, with deities.
He sprinkles with every food,
for the securing of every food.

He sprinkles [him] from the front facing west;
for food is eaten from the front towards the back.
He sprinkles from the head;
for food is eaten from the head.
He lets it stream down up to the mouth;
he places food into him right from the mouth.
"With the universal sovereignty of Agni I sprinkle you," he says;
this indeed is Agni's unction;
with that very [power] he sprinkles him.
"With the universal sovereignty of Indra I sprinkle you," he says;

faculty he thereby places in him.
"With the universal sovereignty of Bṛhaspati I sprinkle you," he says;
Bṛhaspati is the brahman among the gods;
with brahman he sprinkles him.
They bring the soma cups and the offering portions to the priests;
that other realm he makes full of food through them.
And [they bring] the liquor cups and the non-offering portions to the racers;
this realm he makes full of food through them.
He is sprinkled with both;
the racers make a scuffle,

for the securing of faculty.
They chant the praise with unexpressed [verses] at the morning pressing;
Prajāpati is unexpressed;
for the attainment of Prajāpati.
With [verses] containing [the word] "speed" at midday;
speed is food;
food he secures.
With [verses] containing [the word] "Śipiviṣṭa" at the third pressing;
sacrifice is Viṣṇu;
domestic animals are the śipi-flesh;
the sacrifice is established in domestic animals.
The Bṛhat [chant] is the final one;
he brings him to the very end of prosperity.

1.3.9.m:
"You that sit in the waters, you that sit in the ghee," he says;
he is consecrated over waters and over ghee by this.
"You that sit in the sky," he says;
when one becomes wealthier,
they say of him: "He has reached the sky";
he reaches the sky by this.
"You that sit on earth, you that sit in the intermediate space," he says;
he is consecrated over these realms by this.
Therefore, the performer of the Vājapeya descends before no one;
for he is consecrated, as it were, over the deities.
"You that sit in heaven," he says;
when one becomes wealthier,
they say of him: "He has reached heaven";
he reaches heaven by this.
"Which cups belong to the five peoples," he says;
he is consecrated over the five peoples by this.
"The sap of the waters, highest vigor," he says;
he is consecrated over the sap of the waters by this.
"Brought together with the sun's ray," he says, for the sake of clear brightness.

1.3.10.m:
They gave it back to them.
Therefore, [the rite] is performed for the ancestors on the day before.
When he performs [it] for the ancestors on the day before,
having redeemed the sacrifice from the ancestors, the sacrificer stretches it out.
"To Soma accompanied by the ancestors, svadhā, reverence!" he says;
he secures the drink of soma from the father.
For a father, when dying, does not say: "Here is the drink of soma."
The drink of soma is faculty;
faculty, the drink of soma, he secures;
with that faculty he attains a second wife.

1.4.2.m:
Bestow upon us wealth winning prize-food, rich in good heroes,
praiseworthy, glorious, great;
in whom horses, bulls, oxen,
barren cows, and rams are let loose, sacrificed,
to the drinker of sweet drink, soma-backed, the disposer,
to Agni generate from the heart a pleasing thought.
For separately measured has been your seat ordained by the gods;
may you two not commingle in the highest heaven.
Thou art surā, foaming; this is soma;
harm me not as thou enterest thy own womb.

1.4.4.m:
for victory, for glory,
for vigor, for offspring," he says.
Domestic animals indeed are wealth;
he makes the domestic animals rest for him.
"May the two springs of Sarasvatī kindle you," he says.
The Ṛg and Sāman indeed are the two springs of Sarasvatī;
with the Ṛg and Sāman indeed he kindles him.
"You are the sovereign, you are the far-shining," he says.
The Rathantara indeed is the sovereign;
the Bṛhat is the far-shining.

1.4.5.m:
They perform on [verses] addressed to the Maruts;
by that indeed they do not depart from the midday pressing.
They draw [soma] into the Hotṛ's cup;
the Hotṛ recites along.
In the middle indeed he connects the sacrifice.
For whom soma remains over at the midday pressing,
it remains over as desiring the third pressing of the Ādityas.
The sāman is the Gaurīvita;
the Gaurīvita indeed is redundant,
redundant is what remains over from a pressing.

[It is] for the appeasement of the redundant.
"Truly you are great, O Sūrya"—they perform [with this].
With whatever desire of the Ādityas' pressing it remains over,
with that very desire they make him prosper.
The sāman is the Gaurīvita;
by that indeed they do not depart from the midday pressing.
The stoma is the seventeenfold;
by that indeed they do not depart from the third pressing.
They draw [soma] into the Hotṛ's cup;
the Hotṛ recites along.

1.4.6.m:
The opening verses are addressed to the Maruts;
the Maruts indeed are the unconquered abode of the gods;
he strives in the unconquered abode of the gods.
Both the Bṛhat and Rathantara [sāmans] are used.
This [earth] is the Rathantara;
that [sky] is the Bṛhat.
By these two indeed he separates him [the rival]:
from speech and mind,
from exhalation and inhalation,
from sky and earth.
